cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Ghost Lows AID, please cite the software (this file) and Ghost Lows, whose
  fault suite it uses. Co-authors: Pranav Reddy and Cohen Ferrari.
title: "Ghost Lows AID: CGM sensor faults inside open-source automated insulin delivery"
type: software
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-29"
authors:
  - given-names: Pranav
    family-names: Reddy
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-5767-5438"
    affiliation: "University of California, Santa Barbara"
  - given-names: Cohen
    family-names: Ferrari
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-8060-9907"
    affiliation: "University of California, Santa Barbara"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/pkarakala/ghost-lows-aid"
abstract: >-
  Continuous glucose monitors (CGMs) can report false lows, for example when the wearer lies
  on the sensor (compression lows), and other faults such as dropouts, stale readings and
  drift. Automated insulin delivery acts on these readings. Ghost Lows AID injects the Ghost
  Lows fault suite online into unmodified open-source closed-loop algorithms (OpenAPS oref0
  first) in a virtual cohort, and measures the harm each fault causes on true glucose against
  the identical run without the fault. Research software; not a medical device.
keywords:
  - continuous glucose monitoring
  - CGM
  - automated insulin delivery
  - closed loop
  - artificial pancreas
  - OpenAPS
  - oref0
  - sensor faults
  - pressure-induced sensor attenuation
  - compression artifacts
  - compression artefacts
  - type 1 diabetes
  - "UVA/Padova simulator"
  - Python
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Ghost Lows: an open benchmark for real-time CGM low-glucose alarms under sensor faults"
